#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:749
msgid "Stability guarantees"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:752
msgid ""
"build-std enables a much greater array of configurations of the standard "
"library to exist and be produced by stable toolchains than the single "
"configuration that is distributed today."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:756
msgid ""
"It is not feasible for the Rust project to test every combination of profile "
"configuration, Cargo feature, target and standard library crate. As such, "
"the stability of build-std as a mechanism must be separated from the "
"stability guarantees which apply to configurations of the standard library "
"it enables."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:761
msgid ""
"For example, while a stable build-std mechanism may permit the standard "
"library to be built for a tier three target, the Rust project continues to "
"make no commitments or guarantees that the standard library for that target "
"will function correctly or build at all. Even on a tier one target, the Rust "
"project cannot test every possible variation of the standard library that "
"build-std enables."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:768
msgid ""
"The tier of a target no longer determines the possibility of using the "
"standard library, but rather the level of support provided for the standard "
"library on the target."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:772
msgid ""
"Cargo and Rust project documentation will clearly document the "
"configurations which are tested upstream and are guaranteed to work. Any "
"other configurations are supported on a strictly best-effort basis. The Rust "
"project may later choose to provide more guarantees for some well-tested "
"configurations (e.g. enabling sanitisers). This documentation need not go "
"into detail about the exact compilation flags used in a configuration - for "
"example, \"the release profile with the address sanitizer is tested to "
"work\" would be sufficient."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:780
msgid ""
"There are also no guarantees about the exact configuration of the standard "
"library. Over time, the standard library built by build-std could be changed "
"to be closer to that of the pre-built standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:784
msgid ""
"Additionally, there are no guarantees that the build environment required "
"for the standard library will not change over time (e.g. new minimum "
"versions of system packages or C toolchains, etc)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:788
msgid ""
"Building the standard library crates in the sysroot without requiring "
"`RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP` is intended for enabling the standard library to be built "
"with a stable toolchain and stable compiler flags, despite that the standard "
"library uses unstable features in its source code, not as a general "
"mechanism for bypassing Rust's stability mechanisms."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:794
msgid "Drawbacks"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:797
msgid "There are some drawbacks to build-std:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:799
msgid ""
"build-std overlaps with the initial designs and ideas for opaque "
"dependencies in Cargo, thereby introducing a risk of constraining or "
"conflicting with the eventual complete design for opaque dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:803
msgid "Rationale and alternatives"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:806
msgid ""
"This section aims to justify all of the decisions made in the proposed "
"design from [_Proposal_](#proposal) and discuss why alternatives were not "
"chosen."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:809
msgid "Why put `build-std` in the Cargo config?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:812
msgid ""
"There are various alternatives to putting `build-std` in the Cargo "
"configuration:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:814
msgid ""
"Cargo could continue to use an explicit command-line flag to enable build-"
"std, such as the current `-Zbuild-std` (stabilised as `--build-std`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:817
msgid ""
"This approach is proven to work, as per the current unstable implementation, "
"but has a poor user experience, requiring an extra argument to every "
"invocation of Cargo with almost every subcommand of Cargo."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:821
msgid ""
"However, this approach does not lend itself to use with other future and "
"current Cargo features. Additional flags would be required to enable Cargo "
"features (like today's `-Zbuild-std-features`) and would still necessarily "
"be less fine-grained than being able to enable features on individual "
"standard library crates. Similarly for public/private dependencies or "
"customising the profile for the standard library crates."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:828
msgid ""
"build-std could be enabled or disabled in the `Cargo.toml`. However, under "
"which conditions the standard library is rebuilt is better determined by the "
"user of Cargo, rather than the package being built."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:832
msgid ""
"A user may want to never rebuild the standard library so as to avoid "
"invalidating the guarantees of their qualified toolchain, or may want to "
"rebuild unconditionally to further optimise the standard library for their "
"known deployment platform, or may only want to rebuild as necessary to "
"ensure the build will succeed. All of these rationale can apply to the same "
"crate in different circumstances, so it doesn't make sense for a crate to "
"decide this once in its `Cargo.toml`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:840
msgid ""
"It would be a waste of resources if a dependency declared that it must "
"always rebuild the standard library when the pre-built crate would be "
"sufficient and this could not be overridden. It is also unclear how to "
"aggregate different configurations of the `build-std` key from different "
"crates in the dependency graph into a single value."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:846
msgid ""
"While using `build-std` key in the Cargo configuration shares some of the "
"downsides of using an explicit flag - not having a natural extension point "
"for other Cargo options exposed to dependencies - [_Standard library "
"dependencies_](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3875) addresses these "
"concerns."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:851 src/3874-build-std-always.md:864
#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:874 src/3874-build-std-always.md:882
#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:897 src/3874-build-std-always.md:922
#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:934 src/3874-build-std-always.md:957
#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:967 src/3874-build-std-always.md:984
#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1003 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1019
#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1414 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1427
#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1436 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1462
#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1504 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1515
msgid "↩ [_Proposal_](#proposal)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:853
msgid "Why accept `never` as a value for `build-std`?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:856
msgid ""
"The user can specify `never` (the default value) if they prefer which will "
"never rebuild the standard library. rustc will still return an error when "
"the user's target-modifiers do not match the pre-built standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:860
msgid ""
"The `never` value is useful particularly for qualified toolchains where "
"rebuilding the standard library may invalidate the testing that the "
"qualified toolchain has undergone."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:866
msgid ""
"Why add `build-std` to the `[target.<triple>]` and `[target.<cfg>]` sections?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:869
msgid ""
"Supporting `build-std` as a key of both `[build]` and `[target]` sections "
"allows the greatest flexibility for the user. The overhead of rebuilding the "
"standard library may not be desirable in general but would be required when "
"building on targets which do not ship a pre-built standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:876
msgid "Why does `[target]` take precedence over `[build]` for `build-std`?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:879
msgid ""
"`[target]` configuration is necessarily more narrowly scoped so it makes "
"sense for it to override a global default in `[build]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:884
msgid "Why have a manual \"always\" option instead of a \"when-needed\" mode?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:887
msgid ""
"Always using a locally-built standard library avoids the complexity "
"associated with an automatic build-std mechanism while still being useful "
"for users of tier three targets. By leaving an automatic mechanism for a "
"later RFC, fewer of the technical challenges of build-std need to be "
"addressed all at once."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:892
msgid ""
"Having an opt-in mechanism initially, such as `build-std.when = \"always\"`, "
"allows for early issues with build-std to be ironed out without potentially "
"affecting more users like an automatic mechanism. Later proposals will "
"extend the `build-std` option with an automatic mechanism."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:899
msgid "Why does \"always\" rebuild in release profile?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:902
msgid ""
"The release profile most closely matches the existing pre-built standard "
"library, which has proven itself suitable for a majority of use cases."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:905
msgid ""
"With `build-std.when = \"always\"`, it is intended that build-std be a drop-"
"in replacement for std, and so should act the same by default. By minimising "
"the differences between a newly-built std and a pre-built std, there is less "
"chance of the user experiencing bugs or unexpected behaviour from the well-"
"tested and supported pre-built std. Keeping the newly-built std and pre-"
"built std as close as possible reduces the scope of this proposal for the "
"library team."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:912
msgid ""
"Later proposals will extend the `build-std` option with customised standard "
"library builds that use the user's profile (see [_`build-std.when = "
"\"compatible\"` and `build-std.when = \"match-profile\"`_](#build-stdwhen--"
"compatible-and-build-stdwhen--match-profile)). These later proposals are "
"intended to support more niche cases like enabling target modifier flags "
"that require the entire crate graph to agree on - due to ABI "
"incompatibility, for example - and where it wouldn't be appropriate for that "
"to be the default for std. The project could ship pre-built standard "
"libraries for those cases, but this would end up with an combinatorial "
"explosion of pre-built standard libraries."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:924
msgid "Why add `build-std.crates`?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:927
msgid ""
"Not all standard library crates will build on all targets. In a `no_std` "
"project for a tier three target, `build-std.crates` gives the user the "
"ability to limit which crates are built to those they know they need and "
"will build successfully."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:931
msgid ""
"_See [Standard library dependencies\\*](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/"
"pull/3875) for an alternative to `build-std.crates`._"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:936
msgid "Why use the lockfile of the `rust-src` component?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:939
msgid ""
"Using different dependency versions for the standard library would "
"invalidate the upstream testing of the standard library. In particular, some "
"crates use unstable APIs when included as a dependency of the standard "
"library meaning that there is a high risk of build breakage if any package "
"version is changed."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:944
msgid ""
"Using the lockfile included in the `rust-src` component guarantees that the "
"same dependency versions are used as in the pre-built standard library. As "
"the standard library does not re-export types from its dependencies, this "
"will not affect interoperability with the same dependencies of different "
"versions used by the user's crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:950
msgid ""
"Using the lockfile does prevent Cargo from resolving the standard library "
"dependencies to newer patch versions that may contain security fixes. "
"However, this is already impossible with the pre-built standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:954
msgid ""
"See [_Why vendor the standard library's dependencies?_](#why-vendor-the-"
"standard-librarys-dependencies)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:959
msgid "Why not build the standard library in incremental?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:962
msgid ""
"The standard library sources are not intended to be modified locally, "
"similarly to those Cargo fetches from `registry` or `git` sources. "
"Incremental compilation would only add a compilation time overhead for any "
"package sources which do not change."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:969
msgid "Why not produce a `dylib` for the standard library?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:972
msgid ""
"The standard library supports being built as both a `rlib` and a `dylib` and "
"both are shipped as part of the `rust-std` component. As the `dylib` does "
"not contain a metadata hash, it can be rebuilt unnecessarily when toolchain "
"versions change (e.g. switching between stable and nightly and back). The "
"`dylib` is only linked against when `-Cprefer-dynamic` is used. build-std "
"will initially be conservative and not include the `dylib` and `-Cprefer-"
"dynamic` would fallback to static compilation."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:982
msgid ""
"[_Build both `dylib` and `rlib` variants of the standard library_](#build-"
"both-dylib-and-rlib-variants-of-the-standard-library)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:986
msgid ""
"Why use the pre-built standard library for procedural macros and build "
"scripts in cross-compile mode?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:989
msgid ""
"Procedural macros always run on the host and need to be built with a "
"configuration that are compatible with the host toolchain's Cargo and rustc, "
"limiting the potential customisations of the standard library that would be "
"valid. There is little advantage to using a custom standard library with "
"procedural macros, as they are not part of the final output artifact and "
"anywhere they can run already have a toolchain with host tools and a pre-"
"built standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:997
msgid ""
"Build scripts similarly always run on the host and thus would require "
"building the standard library again for the host. There is little advantage "
"to doing this as build scripts are not part of the final output artifact. "
"Build scripts do not respect `RUSTFLAGS` which could result in target "
"modifier mismatches if rebuilding the standard library does respect "
"`RUSTFLAGS`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1005
msgid ""
"Why use the pre-built standard library for procedural macros and build "
"scripts in host mode?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1008
msgid ""
"Unlike when in cross-compile mode, if Cargo is in host mode (i.e. `--target` "
"is not provided), the standard library built by build-std could "
"hypothetically be used for procedural macros and build scripts without "
"additional recompilations of the standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1013
msgid ""
"However, as with [cross-compile mode](#why-use-the-pre-built-standard-"
"library-for-procedural-macros-and-build-scripts-in-cross-compile-mode), "
"there is little advantage to using a customised standard library for "
"procedural macros or build scripts, and both would require limitations on "
"the customisations possible with build-std in order to guarantee "
"compatibility with the compiler or build script, respectively."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1021
msgid ""
"Should target specifications own knowledge of which standard library crates "
"are supported?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1024
msgid ""
"It is much simpler to record this information in a target's specification "
"than build this information into Cargo or to try and match on the target's "
"cfg values in the standard library's `build.rs` and set a cfg that Cargo "
"could read."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1028
msgid ""
"Target specifications have typically been considered part of the compiler "
"and there has been hesitation to have target specs be the source of truth "
"for information like standard library support, as this is the domain of the "
"library team and ought to be owned by the standard library (such as in the "
"standard library's `build.rs`). However, with appropriate processes and sync "
"points, there is no reason why the target specification could not be "
"primarily maintained by the compiler team but in close coordination with "
"library and other relevant teams."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1037 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1152
msgid ""
"↩ [_Standard library crate stability_](#standard-library-crate-stability)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1039
msgid "Why introduce `standard_library_support`?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1042
msgid ""
"Attempting to compile the standard library crates may fail for some targets "
"depending on which standard library crates that target intends to support. "
"When enabled, build-std should default to only building those crates that "
"are expected to succeed, and should prevent the user from attempting to "
"build those crates that are expected to fail. This will provide a much "
"improved user experience than attempting to build standard library crates "
"and encountering complex and unexpected compilation failures."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1050
msgid ""
"For example, `no_std` targets often do not support `std` and so should "
"inform the error with a helpful error message that `std` cannot be built for "
"the target rather than attempt to build it and fail with confusing and "
"unexpected errors. Similarly, many `no_std` targets do support `alloc` if a "
"global allocator is provided, but if build-std built `alloc` by default for "
"these targets then it would often be unnecessary and could often fail."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1057
msgid ""
"It is not sufficient to determine which crates should be supported for a "
"target based on its the tier. For example, targets like `aarch64-apple-tvos` "
"are tier three while intending to fully support the standard library. It "
"would be needlessly limiting to prevent build-std from building `std` for "
"this target. However, build-std does provide a stable mechanism to build "
"`std` for this target that did not previously exist, so there must be "
"clarity about what guarantees and level of support is provided by the Rust "
"project:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1065
msgid ""
"Whether a standard library crate is part of the stable interface of the "
"standard library as a whole is determined by the library team and the set of "
"crates that comprise this interface is the same for all targets"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1069
msgid ""
"Whether any given standard library crate can be built with build-std is "
"determined on a per-target basis depending on whether it is intended that "
"the target be able to support that crate"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1073
msgid ""
"Whether the Rust project provide guarantees or support for the standard "
"library on a target is determined by the tier of the target"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1076
msgid ""
"Whether the pre-built standard library is distributed for a target is "
"determined by the tier of the target and which crates it intends to support"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1079
msgid ""
"Which crate is built by default by build-std is determined on a per-target "
"basis"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1082
msgid "For example, consider the following targets:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1084
msgid "`armv7a-none-eabihf`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1086 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1104
#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1119 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1138
msgid ""
"As with any other target, the `std`, `alloc` and `core` crates are stable "
"interfaces to the standard library"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1089 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1122
msgid ""
"It intends to support the `core` and `alloc` crates, which build-std will "
"permit to be built. `std` cannot be built by build-std for this target (on "
"stable)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1093 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1110
msgid ""
"It is a tier three target, so no support or guarantees are provided for the "
"standard library crates"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1096 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1113
msgid ""
"It is a tier three target, so no standard library crates are distributed"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1098 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1132
msgid ""
"`alloc` would not build without a global allocator crate being provided by "
"the user and may not be required by all users, so only `core` will be built "
"by default"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1102
msgid "`aarch64-apple-tvos`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1107
msgid ""
"It intends to support `core`, `alloc` and `std` crates, which build-std will "
"permit to be built"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1115 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1150
msgid "All of `core`, `alloc` and `std` will be built by default"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1117
msgid "`armv7a-none-eabi`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1126
msgid ""
"It is a tier two target, so the project guarantees that the `core` and "
"`alloc` crates will build"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1129
msgid ""
"It is a tier two target, so there are distributed artifacts for the `core` "
"and `alloc` crates"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1136
msgid "`aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1141
msgid ""
"It intends to support the `core`, `alloc` and `std` crates, which build-std "
"will permit to be built"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1144
msgid ""
"It is a tier one target, so the project guarantees that the `core`, `alloc` "
"and `std` will build and that they have been tested"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1147
msgid ""
"It is a tier one target, so there are distributed artifacts for the `core`, "
"`alloc` and `std` crates"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1154
msgid "Why remove `restricted_std`?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1157
msgid ""
"`restricted_std` was originally added as part of a mechanism to enable the "
"standard library to build on all targets (just with stubbed out "
"functionality), however stability is not an ideal match for this use case. "
"rustc will still try to compile unstable code, so this doesn't help ensure "
"the standard library builds on all targets."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1163
msgid ""
"Furthermore, when `restricted_std` applies, users must add `#!"
"[feature(restricted_std)]` to opt-in to using the standard library anyway "
"(conditionally, only for affected targets), and have no mechanism for opting-"
"in on behalf of their dependencies (including first-party crates like "
"`libtest`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1168
msgid ""
"It is still valuable for the standard library to be able to compile on as "
"many targets as possible using the `unsupported` module in its platform "
"abstraction layer, but this mechanism does not use `restricted_std`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1172
msgid "↩ [_`restricted_std`_](#restricted_std)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1174
msgid "Why disallow custom targets?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1177
msgid ""
"While custom targets can be used on stable today, in practice, they are only "
"used on nightly as `-Zbuild-std` would need to be used to build at least "
"`core`. As such, if build-std were to be stabilised, custom targets would "
"become much more usable on stable toolchains. This is undesirable as there "
"are many open questions surrounding the [unstable target-spec-json](https://"
"github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/71009) for custom targets and how they ought "
"to be supported."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1184
msgid ""
"In order to avoid users relying on the unstable format with a stable "
"toolchain, using custom targets with build-std on a stable toolchain is "
"disallowed by Cargo until another RFC can consider all the implications of "
"this thoroughly."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1188
msgid ""
"Similarly, custom targets are destabilised in rustc, as the changes in "
"[_Building the standard library on a stable toolchain_](#building-the-"
"standard-library-on-a-stable-toolchain) could allow the unstable format to "
"be relied upon even with Cargo's prohibition of custom targets."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1193 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1524
msgid "↩ [_Custom targets_](#custom-targets)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1197
msgid "Why prevent rustc from loading root dependencies from the sysroot?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1200
msgid "Loading root dependencies from the sysroot could be a source of bugs."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1202
msgid ""
"For example, if a crate has an explicit dependency on `core` which is newly "
"built, then there will be no `alloc` or `std` builds present. A user could "
"still write `extern crate alloc` and accidentally load `alloc` from the "
"sysroot (compiled with the default profile settings) and consequently `core` "
"from the sysroot, conflicting with the newly build `core`. `extern crate "
"alloc` should only be able to load the `alloc` crate if the crate depends on "
"it in its `Cargo.toml`. A similar circumstance can occur with dependencies "
"like `panic_unwind` that the compiler tries to load itself."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1211
msgid ""
"Dependencies of packages can still be loaded from the sysroot, even with `--"
"no-implicit-sysroot-deps`, to support the circumstance where Cargo uses a "
"pre-built standard library crate (e.g. `$sysroot/lib/rustlib/$target/lib/std."
"rlib`) and needs to load the dependencies of that crate which are also in "
"the sysroot."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1217
msgid ""
"`--no-implicit-sysroot-deps` is a flag rather than default behaviour to "
"preserve rustc's usability when invoked outside of Cargo. For example, by "
"compiler developers when working on rustc."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1221
msgid ""
"`--sysroot=''` is an existing mechanism for disabling the sysroot - this is "
"not used as it remains desirable to load dependencies from the sysroot as a "
"fallback. In addition, rustc uses the sysroot path to find `rust-lld` and "
"similar tools and would not be able to do so if the sysroot were disabled by "
"providing an empty path."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1227 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1245
#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1260
msgid ""
"↩ [_Preventing implicit sysroot dependencies_](#preventing-implicit-sysroot-"
"dependencies)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1229
msgid "Why use `noprelude` with `--extern`?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1232
msgid ""
"Using `noprelude` allows `build-std` to closer match rustc's behaviour when "
"it loads crates from the sysroot. Without `noprelude`, rustc adds crates "
"provided with `--extern` flags to the extern prelude. As a consequence, if a "
"newly-built `alloc` were passed using `--extern alloc=alloc.rlib` then "
"`extern crate alloc` would not be required to use the locally-built `alloc`, "
"but it would be to use the pre-built `alloc` when `--extern alloc=alloc."
"rlib` is not provided. This difference in how a crate is made available to "
"rustc should not be observable to the user as they have not opted into the "
"migration."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1241
msgid ""
"Passing crates without `noprelude` with the existing prelude behaviour has "
"also been a source of [bugs](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/40) in previous `-Zbuild-std` implementations."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1249
msgid "Why use `nounused` with `--extern`?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1252
msgid ""
"The `unused_crate_dependencies` lint triggers when Cargo tells rustc about a "
"dependency with `--extern` and rustc determines that the dependency is never "
"used. This lint can unintentionally trigger for build-std when standard "
"library crates are passed with `--extern` instead of being loaded from the "
"sysroot implicitly (a difference that should not be observable). The "
"`nounused` extern modifier silences the `unused_crate_dependencies` lint for "
"the standard library crates."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1262
msgid "Why not allow the source path for the standard library be customised?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1265
msgid ""
"It is not a goal of this proposal to enable or improve the usability of "
"custom or modified standard libraries."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1268 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1311
#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1324
msgid "↩ [_Vendored `rust-src`_](#vendored-rust-src)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1270
msgid "Why vendor the standard library's dependencies?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1273
msgid ""
"Vendoring the standard library is possible since it currently has its own "
"workspace, allowing the dependencies of just the standard library crates "
"(and not the compiler or associated tools in `rust-lang/rust`) to be easily "
"packaged. Doing so has multiple advantages.."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1278
msgid ""
"Avoid needing to support standard library dependencies in `cargo vendor`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1279
msgid "Avoid needing to support standard library dependencies in `cargo fetch`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1280
msgid ""
"Re-building the standard library does not require an internet connection"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1281
msgid ""
"Standard library dependency versions are fixed to those in the `Cargo.lock` "
"anyway, so initial builds with `build-std` start quicker with these "
"dependencies already available"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1284
msgid ""
"Allow build-std to continue functioning if a `crates.io` dependency is "
"\"yanked\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1286
msgid ""
"This leaves the consequences of a toolchain version using yanked "
"dependencies the same as without this RFC"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1289
msgid "..and few disadvantages:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1291
msgid ""
"A larger `rust-src` component takes up more disk space and takes longer to "
"download"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1293
msgid ""
"If using build-std, these dependencies would have to be downloaded at build "
"time, so this is only an issue if build-std is not used and `rust-src` is "
"downloaded."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1296
msgid ""
"`rustc-src` is currently 3.5 MiB archived and 44 MiB extracted, and if "
"dependencies of the standard library were vendored, then it would be 9.1 MiB "
"archived and 131 MiB extracted."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1299
msgid "Vendored dependencies can't be updated with the latest security fixes"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1300
msgid "This is no different than the pre-built standard library"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1302
msgid ""
"How this affects `crates.io`/`rustup` bandwidth usage or user time spent "
"downloading these crates is unclear and depends on user patterns. If not "
"vendored, Cargo will \"lazily\" download them the first time `build-std` is "
"used but this may happen multiple times if they are cleaned from its cache "
"without upgrading the toolchain version."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1308
msgid ""
"See [_Why use the lockfile of the `rust-src` component?_](#why-use-the-"
"lockfile-of-the-rust-src-component)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1313
msgid "Why not check if `rust-src` has been modified?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1316
msgid ""
"This is in line with other immutable dependency sources (like registry or "
"git). It is also likely that any protections implemented to check that the "
"sources in `rust-src` have not been modified could be trivially bypassed."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1320
msgid ""
"Any crate that depends on `rust-src` having been modified would not be "
"usable when published to crates.io as the required modifications will "
"obviously not be included."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1326
msgid "Why allow building from the sysroot with implied `RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP`?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1329
msgid ""
"Cargo needs to be able to build the standard library crates, which "
"inherently require unstable features. It could set `RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP` "
"internally to do this with a stable toolchain, but this is a bypass "
"mechanism that the project do not want to encourage use of, and as this is a "
"shared requirement with other build systems that wish to build an unmodified "
"standard library and want to work on stable toolchains, it is worth "
"establishing a narrow general mechanism."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1336
msgid ""
"For example, Rust's project goal to enable Rust for Linux to build using "
"only a stable toolchain would require that it be possible to build `core` "
"without nightly."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1340
msgid ""
"It is not sufficient for rustc to special-case the `core`, `alloc` and `std` "
"crate names as, when being built as part of the standard library, "
"dependencies of the standard library also use unstable features and it is "
"not practical to special-case all of these crates."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1345
msgid ""
"↩ [_Building the standard library on a stable toolchain_](#building-the-"
"standard-library-on-a-stable-toolchain)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1347
msgid "Why invert the `mem` feature?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1350
msgid ""
"While \"negative\" features are typically discouraged due to how features "
"unify (e.g. `std` features are preferred to `no_std`): the `mem` feature's "
"current behaviour is the opposite of what is optimal."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1354
msgid ""
"Ideally, a crate should be able to provide alternate memory symbols and "
"disable `compiler_builtins`' symbols for the entire crate graph by enabling "
"a feature (e.g. `std`/`libc` could do this) - this is what an `external-mem` "
"feature enables."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1359 src/3874-build-std-always.md:1374
msgid "↩ [_`compiler-builtins-mem`_](#compiler-builtinsmem)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1361
msgid "Why not use weak linkage for `compiler-builtins/mem` symbols?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1364
msgid ""
"Since [compiler-builtins#411](https://github.com/rust-lang/compiler-builtins/"
"pull/411), the relevant symbols in `compiler_builtins` already have weak "
"linkage. However, it is nevertheless not possible to simply remove the `mem` "
"feature and have the symbols always be present:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1368
msgid ""
"Some targets, such as those based on MinGW, do not have sufficient support "
"for weak definitions (at least with the default linker)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1370
msgid ""
"Weak linkage has precedence over shared libraries and the symbols of a "
"dynamically-linked `libc` should be preferred over `compiler_builtins`'s "
"symbols."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1378
msgid "Why not globally cache builds of the standard library?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1381
msgid ""
"The standard library is no different than regular dependencies in being able "
"to benefit from global caching of dependency builds. It is out-of-scope of "
"this proposal to propose a special-cased mechanism for this that applies "
"only to the standard library. [cargo#5931](https://github.com/rust-lang/"
"cargo/issues/5931) tracks the feature request of intermediate artifact "
"caching in Cargo."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1387
msgid "↩ [_Caching_](#caching)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1391
msgid ""
"Why not link to hosted standard library documentation in generated docs?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1394
msgid ""
"Cargo would need to pass `-Zcrate-attr=\"doc(html_root_url=..)\"` to the "
"standard library crates when building them but doesn't have the required "
"information to know what url to provide. Cargo would require knowledge of "
"the current toolchain channel to build the correct url and doesn't know this."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1399
msgid "↩ [_Generated documentation_](#generated-documentation)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1401
msgid "Unresolved questions"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1404
msgid ""
"The following are aspects of the proposal which warrant further discussion "
"or small details are likely to be bikeshed prior to this part of the RFC's "
"acceptance or stabilisation and aren't pertinent to the overall design:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1408
msgid ""
"What should the `build-std.when` configuration in `.cargo/config.toml` be "
"named?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1411
msgid ""
"What should this configuration option be named? `build-std`? `rebuild-"
"standard-library`?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1416
msgid ""
"What should the \"always\" and \"never\" values of `build-std` be named?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1419
msgid ""
"What is the most intuitive name for the values of the `build-std` setting? "
"`always`? `manual`? `unconditional`?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1422
msgid ""
"`always` combined with the configuration option being named `build-std` - "
"`build-std.when = \"always\"` - is imperfect as it reads as if the standard "
"library will be re-built every time, when it actually just avoids use of the "
"pre-built standard library and caches the newly-built standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1429
msgid "What should `build-std.crates` be named?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1432
msgid ""
"What should this configuration option be named? In particular, `crates` "
"being plural is unintuitive: while it can enable building multiple crates, "
"it is set with the name of only a single crate. `up-to` or `which` might be "
"better."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1438
msgid "Should the standard library inherit RUSTFLAGS?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1441
msgid ""
"Existing designs for _[Opaque dependencies](https://github.com/rust-lang/"
"cargo/issues/3573#issuecomment-3498262549)_ intended that `RUSTFLAGS` would "
"not apply to the opaque dependency."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1444
msgid ""
"`RUSTFLAGS` is an escape hatch for setting rustc flags, and could be used to "
"set a target modifier flag. If the standard library build ignored this "
"variable, then rustc would fail to build the user's project due to "
"incompatible target modifiers. By respecting this escape hatch, the purpose "
"of `RUSTFLAGS` isn't prevented from having its desired effect."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1450
msgid ""
"`RUSTFLAGS` as a mechanism for customising the compilation has overlap with "
"Cargo's profiles - and this proposal's treatment of `RUSTFLAGS` and profiles "
"is deliberately inconsistent:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1454
msgid ""
"Cargo typically does not inspect `RUSTFLAGS`, treating it as a low-level "
"escape hatch that is opaque to Cargo in the implications of the flags it "
"passes. Inheriting the `RUSTFLAGS` variable is consistent with this approach "
"as it remains opaque to Cargo with no special treatment and applies to all "
"rustc invocations. This is contrast with profiles, which are a first-class "
"Cargo concept, and thus can have different behaviours in different contexts "
"when it makes sense to do so."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1464
msgid "Should `rust-src` be a default component?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1467
msgid ""
"Ensuring `rust-src` is a default component reduces friction for users, and "
"CI, who have to otherwise need to install the component manually the first "
"time they use `build-std`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1471
msgid ""
"On the other hand this increases their storage and bandwidth costs, plus "
"bandwidth costs for the project. The impact on usability is limited for the "
"user to once per toolchain as the component persists through updates."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1475
msgid "↩ [_Vendored rust-src_](#vendored-rust-src)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1477
msgid "Should `cargo clean` delete builds of the standard library?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1480
msgid ""
"`cargo clean` could retain builds of the standard library unless explicitly "
"requested. Builds of the standard library are not going change unless the "
"toolchain version has changed. This should be consistent with the treatment "
"of opaque dependencies in `cargo clean` more broadly."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1485
msgid "↩ [_Cargo subcommands_](#cargo-subcommands)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1487
msgid "Prior art"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1490
msgid ""
"See the [_Background_](https://rust-lang.github.io/rfcs/3873-build-std-"
"context.html#background) and [_History_](https://rust-lang.github.io/"
"rfcs/3873-build-std-context.html#history) of the build-std context RFC."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1493
msgid "Future possibilities"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1496
msgid "There are many possible follow-ups to this part of the RFC:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1498
msgid "Adding a shorthand"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1501
msgid ""
"A `build-std = \"always\"` shorthand could be introduced for `build-std."
"when` if it was deemed appropriate."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1506
msgid "Allow reusing sysroot artifacts if available"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1509
msgid ""
"This part of the RFC proposes rebuilding all required crates unconditionally "
"as this fits Cargo's existing compilation model better. However, just "
"building a crate equivalent to one already in the sysroot is inefficient. "
"Cargo could learn when to reuse artifacts in the sysroot when equivalent to "
"ones it intends to build, but this is complex enough to warrant its own "
"proposal if desired."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1517
msgid "Allow custom targets with build-std"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1520
msgid ""
"This would require a decision from the relevant teams on the exact stability "
"guarantees of the target-spec-json format and whether any large changes to "
"the format are desirable prior to broader use."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1526
msgid "Avoid building `panic_unwind` unnecessarily"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1529
msgid ""
"This would require adding a `--print default-unwind-strategy` flag to rustc "
"and using that to avoid building `panic_unwind` if the default is abort for "
"any given target and `panic` is not set in the profile."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1533
msgid "↩ [_Panic strategies_](#panic-strategies)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1535
msgid "Enable local recompilation of special object files/sanitizer runtimes"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1538
msgid ""
"These files are shipped pre-compiled for relevant targets and are not "
"compiled locally. If a user wishes to customise the compilation of these "
"files like the standard library, then there is no mechanism to do so."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1542
msgid "↩ [_Self-contained objects_](#self-contained-objects)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1544
msgid "Allow building `profiler-builtins`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1547
msgid ""
"It may be possible to ship a rustup component with pre-compiled native "
"dependencies of `profiler-builtins` so that build-std can reliably compile "
"the `profiler-builtins` crate regardless of the environment. Alternatively, "
"stability guarantees could be adjusted to set expectations that some parts "
"of the standard library may not build without external system dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1553
msgid ""
"If `profiler-builtins` can be reliably built, then it should be "
"unconditionally included in part of the standard library build."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1556
msgid "↩ [_profiler-builtins_](#profiler-builtins)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1558
msgid "Build both `dylib` and `rlib` variants of the standard library"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1561
msgid ""
"build-std could build both the `dylib` and `rlib` of the standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1563
msgid ""
"↩ [_Why not produce a `dylib` for the standard library?_](#why-not-produce-a-"
"dylib-for-the-standard-library)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1565
msgid ""
"`build-std.when = \"compatible\"` and `build-std.when = \"match-profile\"`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1568
msgid ""
"There are follow-up draft proposals prepared which extend the `build-std."
"when` option with variants that will automatically re-build the standard "
"library only when it strictly necessary for the build to succeed (i.e. when "
"a target modifier changes), and that match the user's profile."
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1573
msgid ""
"These proposals are drafts and are intended only to signpost future "
"direction for this feature, and ensure compatibility with the current "
"proposal:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1576
msgid ""
"[`build-std.when = \"compatible\"`](https://github.com/davidtwco/rfcs/blob/"
"build-std-part-four-compatible/text/0000-build-std-compatible.md)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1577
msgid ""
"[`build-std.when = \"match-profile\"`](https://github.com/davidtwco/rfcs/"
"blob/build-std-part-five-match-profile/text/0000-build-std-match-profile.md)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1582
msgid ""
"↩ [_Why does \"always\" rebuild in release profile?_](#why-does-always-"
"rebuild-in-release-profile)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1584
msgid "Summary of proposed changes"
msgstr ""

#: src/3874-build-std-always.md:1587
msgid ""
"These are each of the changes which would need to be implemented in the Rust "
"toolchain grouped by the project team whose purview the change would fall "
"under:"
msgstr ""

